    "type": "question",
    "label": "83",
    "prompt": "Use the identity $a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2)$ to rationalise the denominator of\n$$\\frac1{1+\\sqrt[3]2}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{1-\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{3}$.\n\nApply the identity with $a=1$ and $b=-\\sqrt[3]2$.",
      "worked": "Let $t=\\sqrt[3]2$. The supplied identity with $a=1$, $b=-t$ gives $(1+t)(1-t+t^2)=1+t^3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac1{1+\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{(1+\\sqrt[3]2)(1-\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4)}\\\\\\frac1{1+\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{1+2}\\\\\\frac1{1+\\sqrt[3]2}&=\\frac{1-\\sqrt[3]2+\\sqrt[3]4}{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
